<CaseStudyProblem
  eyebrow="PROBLEM SELECTION"
  title="Finding the 'Single Point of Failure'"
  description={`As we started to deconstruct the problem, I realized that we were designing for the "Aftermath" of the problem, not the "Source." We found that 7 out of 10 shoppers genuinely want to switch to healthier alternatives at the point of purchase, yet only 1 in 10 actually succeeds in finding one. This "Intent to Action Gap" represents a missed opportunity for clean-label brands & a health catastrophe for the consumer. Our initial discovery revealed that the "exhaustion" wasn't due to a lack of healthy options, but a lack of understanding & trust:`}
 problems={problems}
  client:load
/>

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-10 pb-40">
  <div class="border-l-3 border-zinc-700 pl-6 md:pl-8">
  <p class="text-2xl tracking-wider font-google font-bold text-zinc-50 italic">
    Conclusion: Solve only for Label Literacy
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-zinc-400 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed italic">
    Finally, we agreed that while the other problems dealt with buying or tracking food, the ‘Single Point of Failure’ happened much earlier—at the moment of Decision.
    So, by solving Label Literacy, we empower the user at the exact "Moment of Truth"—the 5 seconds before the product hits the cart. And, it would naturally fix the other problems in the future.

  </p>
  </div>
</section>


<CaseStudyPrologue
  eyebrow="DEFINING THE PROBLEM"
  title="'Health Halo' is killing Indians"
  description={`India is eating itself sick. Over 100 million people struggle with "lifestyle diseases"—like Diabetes and PCOD, caused by ultra-processed foods disguised as "healthy". Consumers are now desperately seeking help. While urban Indians are more "health-conscious" than ever, they are losing the battle against the "Health Halo." We see a massive surge in people trying to make better choices, but they are being stopped by a "Wall of Jargon" on the back of food packaging.`}
  image="/Images/cosmograph.png"
  note="*Representational Graph"
  detail={`So, I had to design a solution to capture this exact, high-intent moment of doubt by translating complex chemical ingredients & nutritional tables into a simple, personalized "Match Score" based on a user’s unique health profile. This provides an opportunity to convert high-friction skepticism into instant, trust-driven conversion. Based on this understanding, I defined the problem statement by asking a simple question:`}
  image="/Images/cosmograph.png"
  client:load
/>

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-10 pt-25 pb-50">
  <div class="border-l-3 border-zinc-700 pl-6 md:pl-8">
  <p class="text-2xl tracking-wider font-google font-bold text-zinc-50">
    The Problem Statement:
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-zinc-400 text-base md:text-2xl leading-relaxed ">
    How might we transform complex label jargon into a personalized, instant, and trustworthy health verdict for busy shoppers—empowering their 'intent to buy healthy' in a matter of seconds?
  </p>
  </div>
</section>

<CaseStudyIdeation
  eyebrow="DESIGN PROCESS & STRATEGY"
  title="Navigating Divergent Ideas"
  description={`Instead of ideating in a silo, we gathered a cross-functional team to brainstorm how to dismantle the "Health Halo". We used a collaborative board to collect ideas on how to turn complex data into instant, actionable insights. The goal was to explore every possible way to bridge the gap between intent and action before narrowing our focus to the most viable path.
  
  Some of the ideas we initially converged on:`}
  images={[
    "/Ideas/idea7.png",
    "/Ideas/idea6.png",
    "/Ideas/idea3.png",
    "/Ideas/idea5.png",
    "/Ideas/idea2.png",
    "/Ideas/idea4.png",
    "/Ideas/idea8.png",
    "/Ideas/idea1.png",
  ]}
  detail={`This collaborative ideation session was essential for mapping out different approaches and evaluating their long-term potential. While many innovative concepts emerged, our next step was to filter them through the lens of feasibility and high immediate user impact.`}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyStrategy
  eyebrow="THE DECISION ARCHITECTURE"
  title="Choosing a Path from 25+ Ideas"
  description="To transition from a chaotic board of ideas to a cohesive product strategy, we established a strict evaluative framework. We needed to ensure our final path was not just creative, but deeply anchored in human psychology, technical reality, and the specific behavioral constraints of our users."
  strategyHeading="1. UX Prioritization"
  strategyDescription="We plotted every concept from our ideation phase against an Impact vs. Feasibility matrix. Our goal was to isolate the highest-leverage solutions that could be realistically built and deployed."
  image="/Images/matrix.png"
  note="*Representational Graph"
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyDecisions
  decisionHeading="2. Deciding Factors"
  decisionDescription="Our approach was simple: Design for the subconscious, not just the interface. We focused on these four deciding factors based on user psychology, emotional states, and behavioral triggers:"
  factors={factors}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyUsers
  personaHeading="3. The Target Personas"
  personaDescription="To design an effective solution, we also had to map our target audience based on their willingness to engage with health data which varied wildly based on their health priority and emotional investment."
  personas={personas}
  detail={`We realized our MVP needed to cater heavily to the Conscious Hustler and Vigilant Optimizer—the segments experiencing the sharpest friction between their intent and the reality of the market. The Passive Buyers were simply not our target audience.`}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyFlow
  eyebrow="UNDERSTANDING USER FLOWS"
  title="Where does Cosmo fits into the picture?"
  description={`Before designing a single interface, we had to answer a critical product question: How do we introduce a health-conscious marketplace without forcing users to completely rewrite their deep-rooted shopping habits?

Modern consumers are deeply conditioned by the hyper-efficiency of quick-commerce giants like Blinkit, Zepto, and Swiggy Instamart. They expect friction-free, instantaneous gratification. If our solution required them to adopt an entirely new, tedious mental model, the product would face immediate abandonment.

Cosmo was built not to disrupt existing user behavior, but to elegantly piggyback on it.`}
  flowHeading="1. Dissecting the Current Q-Commerce Flow"
  flowDescription="In the current Indian retail landscape, platforms like Blinkit and Zepto have automated the user’s shopping ritual into a highly efficient, subconscious loop. However, this hyper-efficiency creates major friction for healthy eating:"
  steps={flowSteps}
  client:load
/>

<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-5 sm:px-10 py-25">
  <div class="border-l-3 border-zinc-700 pl-6 md:pl-8">
  <p class="text-2xl tracking-wider font-google font-bold text-zinc-50">
    The Design Challenge:
  </p>
  <p class="mt-4 text-zinc-400 text-base md:text-lg leading-relaxed ">
   If we intercept this loop with a slow, educational tool that lectures the user on ingredients, we break the velocity they crave. Cosmo needed to match the exact speed and convenience of a 10-minute delivery app while fundamentally upgrading the quality of what goes into the cart.
  </p>
  </div>
</section>


<CaseStudySolutions
  eyebrow="FINAL PRODUCT DESIGN"
  title="The Solution: Introducing Cosmo"
  description={`To bridge the gap between health intent and retail reality without breaking established consumer habits, we designed Cosmo. By focusing on real-world utility, Cosmo integrates into a user's routine, providing instant confidence without asking them to learn a new habit. It solves a single major frustration: the anxiety, second-guessing, and confusion that happens when trying to buy healthy groceries.


Instead of an abstract shopping app, Cosmo is a comprehensive ecosystem powered by three core pillars:`}
 solutions={solutions}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyDesigns
  eyebrow="A) PRODUCT SCANNER"
  title="Bridging Intent to Action through Instant Verdict"
  description={`To serve a user who values proactive health over panic, I had to design the core scanning and search flows, to turn complex nutrition data into instant, confident decisions. By streamlining the entire experience, we ensured that every scan and search seamlessly connects the user's health goals with better everyday choices.`}

  steps={[
    {
      label: "A) Accommodating Varied Scanning Scenarios",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Low lighting or blurry camera in real-world settings.
      • Torn, wrinkled, or poorly printed barcodes that fail to read.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: "A unified scanner that automatically detects the product or barcode, paired with intuitive, thumb-zone toggle for manual overrides and a text-search option.",
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "It respcts high-velocity user behaviour.",

      image: "/Flows/flow1.png",
    },

    {
      label: "B) Navigating Product Match Discrepancies",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Discrepancies when a scan matches multiple similar variants.
      • Guessing incorrectly undermines accuracy & trust.`,
      title2: "Iteration-1",
      description2: "An error screen asking the user to rescan when the system fails to find a single, definitive match.",
      title3: "Iteration-2",
      description3: "Horizontal carousel of top 3 matches, requiring the user to compare & select the correct one before proceeding.",

      image: "/Flows/flow2.png",
    },

    {
      label: "B) Navigating Product Match Discrepancies",
      title1: "Iteration-3 (Final Solution)",
      description1: `• A Smart Confirmation screen that displays the highest-confidence match which auto-redirects to the verdict page.
      • If the user identifies a mismatch during this window, a single tap immediately reveals a list of alternative options.`,
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: `• Prioritizes speed, resolving 90% of cases without user intervention.
      • Effectively balances system speed with user agency, ensuring that the flow is never interrupted.`,

      image: "/Flows/flow1.png",
    },

    {
      label: "C) Resolving 'Product Not Found' into Community Value",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Momentum blocked, triggers immediate disappointment.
      • Lose confidence in the app's reliability.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: "An overlay acknowledging that the user has found something unique with a streamlined camera interface, that requests quick snapshots to fix the database gap.",
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "Transforms a moment of UX friction into a psychological positive.",

      image: "/Flows/flow3.png",
    },
  ]}
  client:load
/>


<CaseStudyDesigns
  eyebrow="B) RATING & HEALTH SCORE"
  title="Converting Label Insights into Confident Decisions"
  description={`The next step was to design an intuitive product description and rating screen that instantly translates complex data into an empowering, clear health score that deepens label literacy while immediately surfacing highly compatible, healthier alternatives, letting users swap and upgrade their cart with a single tap.`}

  steps={[
    {
      label: "A) Translating raw data into personalized insights",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Nutrition labels are complex & text-heavy leading to immediate visual fatigue & analytical paralysis.
      • Abstract metrics offer zero context & personal relevance.`,
      title2: "Iteration-1",
      description2: "A vertical list view of color-coded, granular, individual metrics with personalized insights categorized into negatives & positives.",
      title3: "Why it didn't work?",
      description3: "No at a glance unified verdict, scattered the user's attention.",

      image: "/Flows/flow2.png",
    },

     {
      label: "A) Translating raw data into personalized insights",
      title1: "Iteration-2 (Final Solution)",
      description1: "An organized grid of snackable cards that prioritizes only key insights tailored to the personal context linked to the user's health profile & goals while leaving the detailed ingredients as expandable bottom drawers utilizing progressive disclosure. Also, instantly contextualizing severity using color semantics.",
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: `• Satisfies the user's need for at a glance verdict by offering hierarchy-driven scannability.
      • Shifts user emotion from confusion to confident validation with personalized intuitive insights.`,

      image: "/Flows/flow1.png",
    },

    {
      label: "B) Overcoming the 'Abstract Score' Indifference",
      title1: "Challenge",
      description1: `The "One-Size-Fits-All" Score, treats nutrition as a monolith diluting unique risks & goals.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: `A clear & vibrant rating card showing a 1-to-10 linear scale, combining an explicit score with a highly scannable text conclusion, supported by a reactive mascot and custom context based on user health profiles.`,
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "The interface communicates a comprehensive health score in under a second and is highly receptive.",

      image: "/Flows/flow1.png",
    },

    {
      label: "C) Earning Trust Through Transparent Score Logic",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Risk of creating a false expectation of clinical lab backing.
      • Lose trust in the app's credibility.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: "Presenting the rating as a personalized advisory score along with a prominent, interactive info-trigger that opens a conversational breakdown of the score logic.",
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "Kills skepticism, & builds long-term brand integrity.",

      image: "/Flows/flow3.png",
    },

    {
      label: "D) Preventing Churn and Keeping Cart Momentum",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Unhealthy verdicts trigger emotional restriction & disappointment.
      • Page dead-ends cause immediate cart abandonment.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: `Embedding a contextual carousel of healthier "Smart Swap" alternatives directly below the negative product indicators, enabling frictionless, single-tap cart upgrades.`,
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "Positive reinforcement keeps the purchase loop moving.",

      image: "/Flows/flow3.png",
    },
  ]}
  client:load
/>


<CaseStudyDesigns
  eyebrow="C) MARKETPLACE EXPERIENCE"
  title="Turning Standard Browsing into Informed Grocery Shopping"
  description={`The next step was to re-architect the marketplace navigation and product feeds, shifting from generic product layouts to a tailored shopping experience that matches individual health parameters at a single glance.`}

  steps={[
    {
      label: "A) Reorganizing Browse Categories for health intent.",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Generic, diet-blind categories force users to blindly browse through unsuitable products.
      • Repetitive manual filtering causes immediate user fatigue.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: `Elevating custom, profile-driven "Smart Categories" that automatically pre-filter aisles based on the user's specific health profile.`,
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "Eliminates navigational friction, accelerating the path-to-cart.",

      image: "/Flows/flow3.png",
    },
    {
      label: "B) Minimizing click-fatigue through self-contained cards",
      title1: "Challenges",
      description1: `• Hidden nutritional trade-offs makes product evaluation & comparison exhausting.
      • Blind browsing leads to cognitive friction.`,
      title2: "Solution",
      description2: `Optimizing the primary discovery layer by moving critical, personalized nutritional insights directly onto the product cards within the search results and category feeds.`,
      title3: "Why this works?",
      description3: "Eliminates clicking friction & accelerates checkout velocity.",

      image: "/Flows/flow3.png",
    },
  ]}
  client:load
/>

<CaseStudyVideo
